    "https://jobs.anu.edu.au/jobs/postdoctoral-fellow-canberra-act-act-australia-3db3351a-c5fa-402e-98b6-9ce5dc4c4fca": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Privacy Statement People & Culture Information for applicants Salaries & benefits Respect and Inclusion Aboriginal & Torres Strait Islander employment Applicant Login Kiosk mode Postdoctoral Fellow Postdoctoral Fellow 563947 Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia, 2601 Fixed Term Closing at: Nov 15 2026 - 23:55 AEDT Add to favourites View favourites Position Description Classification: Academic Level A Salary package: $91,546 - $114,906 per annum plus 17% Superannuation Terms: Full-time, Fixed Term (up to 2 years) Contribute to an innovative international research project examining the International Court of Justice (ICJ) and its evolving role as the ‘World Court’, using cutting-edge empirical and sociolegal research methods. Undertake rich, interdisciplinary research spanning archival research, qualitative interviews and novel dataset development, with opportunities to co-author academic publications and contribute to high-impact research outputs. Join an international and interdisciplinary research network , working closely with Dr Esmé Shirlow (ANU) and Dr Florian Grisel (CNRS) and engaging with leading academics, international lawyers, government officials and practitioners connected to the ICJ. In 1946, ANU was founded to help rebuild a changing world. We’re still at it, 80 years later. For generations, our people have turned curiosity into ideas that have shaped Australia and our region. Learn more about ANU, our history and the community that has shaped us: ANU at 80 Position overview The ANU Law School is consistently ranked among the world’s leading law schools. For over 65 years, it has stood at the forefront of legal education and impact as Australia’s national law school, earning global recognition for scholarly excellence and shaping legal and policy debates. With historical strengths in public and international law, and a growing international reputation in environmental law, private and corporate law, and human rights law, the ANU Law School shapes law and policy and prepares graduates for a rapidly evolving legal landscape. This position supports an ARC Discovery Project grant entitled “Becoming a ‘World Court’? Globalisation and Judicialisation at the International Court of Justice” (2026–2029), contributing to the project’s goal of producing a large-scale empirical and sociolegal study of the International Court of Justice (ICJ) as a global institution. The Postdoctoral Fellow is expected to undertake research across the project’s empirical, archival, and analytical components. The allocation of time to each area will be discussed with the position supervisor annually and be reflective of the external funding conditions that support the appointment, the appointee’s research agenda, and opportunities within the ANU School of Law. The Postdoctoral Fellow may also be required to support project coordination activities, contribute to the organisation of workshops and the project’s Research Hub, and assist in the supervision of higher degree by research (HDR) students, and contribute cooperatively to the overall intellectual life of the College and University. For further information please contact Professor Esme Shirlow T: +61 2 6125 0810, E: Esme.Shirlow@anu.edu.au. The University actively encourages applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. For more information on employment opportunities, contact our Indigenous Employment Consultant on indigenous.employment@anu.edu.au ANU values diversity and inclusion and is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to people of all backgrounds and identities.  For more information about staff equity at ANU, click here . For enquiries about the application process, or if you require reasonable adjustment to apply for this role, please contact the hiring manager on the above listed details. Application information In order to apply for this role please make sure that you upload the following documents: A statement addressing the selection criteria. A current curriculum vitae (CV) which includes the names and contact details of at least three referees (preferably including a current or previous supervisor). If your CV does not include referees you can complete these online when prompted in the application form. Other documents, if required. Applications which do not address the selection criteria may not be considered for the position. Please note: The preferred candidate must have rights to live and work in Australia, and will be required to complete mandatory pre-employment declarations and background checks relevant to the role they have applied for, in accordance with the Background Checking Procedure and the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence . Recommended Jobs Lead Optical Specialist Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Ideal for an experienced optical engineer, this position provides the chance to shape cutting-edge scientific and space technologies while supporting world-leading research and innovation. Mechanical Engineering Specialist Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Join the Advanced Instrumentation and Technology Centre (AITC) to provide specialist mechanical engineering expertise supporting the design, development, validation and maintenance of advanced astronomical instrumentation for world-leading research. Senior Consultant HR Systems Operations Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Oversee operational support for key HR systems, ensuring high-quality service delivery to HR and Payroll stakeholders Associate Professor/ Professor of Music Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 An opportunity is available for an outstanding scholar to take up a leadership position at the ANU School of Music working within a collaborative team with world-class researchers and educators. Research Assistant / Research Engineer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Work with state-of-the-art techniques on AI and Robotics, in collaboration with researchers and practitioners from universities and industry. Research Officer (Peakall) Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Research Officer to perform high quality plant DNA/RNA extraction, library preparation for sequencing and molecular laboratory research support."
    },
    "https://jobs.anu.edu.au/jobs/lecturer-senior-lecturer-in-economics-canberra-act-act-australia": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Economics",
      "description": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Economics - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Privacy Statement People & Culture Information for applicants Salaries & benefits Respect and Inclusion Aboriginal & Torres Strait Islander employment Applicant Login Kiosk mode Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Economics Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Economics 561517 Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia, 2601 Tenure Track Closing at: Dec 6 2026 - 23:55 AEDT Add to favourites View favourites Position Description Classification: Academic Level B or C Salary package: $124,638 - $166,338 per annum plus 17% superannuation Terms: Full time, Tenure Track Position overview The Research School of Economics at the Australian National University (ANU) is seeking expressions of interest from highly qualified candidates for the position of Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Economics specialising in econometrics or empirical economic research, however any field of specialisation will be considered. The successful candidates must currently hold a PhD or have submitted a PhD in the discipline of Economics. The successful applicants will complement and extend existing research and teaching strengths within the ANU Research School of Economics and will be committed to high-quality teaching and excellence in research. Several tenure track positions are available. Tenure track appointments will be for an initial period of seven years, with the option of renewal or conversion to an ongoing position at the end of the initial employment agreement in accordance with the ANU Enterprise Agreement. Position location The ANU Research School of Economics is one of Australia’s premier academic institutions and provides an outstanding environment for economic research, teaching, and research training. The successful applicant will join a strong team of academics who publish in top international journals, hold external competitive grants, and receive recognitions for their sustained pursuit of educational and research excellence. The College The ANU College of Business and Economics (CBE) seeks to advance knowledge through high quality teaching and research. We contribute to associated professions, industry and government by conducting world-class, long-term strategic research, and through the provision of a range of specialised undergraduate and postgraduate degree programs. As a part of Australia's leading university, CBE provides an excellent educational experience for our students. Our global alumni network reaches the world's leading companies, policy agencies and universities. We strongly believe that the way in which we operate is defined by our Values and Behaviours and encourage you to apply if you are looking for a workplace that encourages respect, collegiality and excellence. The University actively encourages applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. For more information on employment opportunities, contact our Indigenous Employment Consultant on indigenous.employment@anu.edu.au ANU values diversity and inclusion and is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to those of all backgrounds and identities. For more information about staff equity at ANU, visit https://services.anu.edu.au/human-resources/respect-inclusion For more information, please contact Andy Martin, School Manager: andrew.martin@anu.edu.au Application information Job market paper/recent publication A cover letter detailing your motivation to apply to the RSE, ANU A current curriculum vitae (CV) Letters of reference (3) Please submit your application via Econ Job Market: https://econjobmarket.org/positions/12610 . All applicants will be considered for both academic Level B and C positions. Applications which do not address the selection criteria may not be considered for the position. Please note: The preferred candidate will be required to complete mandatory pre-employment declarations and background checks relevant to the role they have applied for, in accordance with the Background Checking Procedure and the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence . Recommended Jobs Lead Optical Specialist Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Ideal for an experienced optical engineer, this position provides the chance to shape cutting-edge scientific and space technologies while supporting world-leading research and innovation. Mechanical Engineering Specialist Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Join the Advanced Instrumentation and Technology Centre (AITC) to provide specialist mechanical engineering expertise supporting the design, development, validation and maintenance of advanced astronomical instrumentation for world-leading research. Senior Consultant HR Systems Operations Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Oversee operational support for key HR systems, ensuring high-quality service delivery to HR and Payroll stakeholders Associate Professor/ Professor of Music Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 An opportunity is available for an outstanding scholar to take up a leadership position at the ANU School of Music working within a collaborative team with world-class researchers and educators. Research Assistant / Research Engineer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Work with state-of-the-art techniques on AI and Robotics, in collaboration with researchers and practitioners from universities and industry. Research Officer (Peakall) Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Research Officer to perform high quality plant DNA/RNA extraction, library preparation for sequencing and molecular laboratory research support. Candidate Details This website uses cookies. We use cookies to personalise content such as job recommendations, and to analyse our traffic. You consent to our cookies if you click \"I Accept\". If you click on \"I Do Not Accept\", then we will not use cookies but you may have a deteriorated user experience. You can change your settings by clicking on the Settings link on the top right of the device I do not accept I accept"
    },
    "https://jobs.anu.edu.au/jobs/casual-sessional-academic-coral-bell-school-of-asia-pacific-affairs-canberra-act-act-australia-5f781f4c-7606-4017-91c1-3f5e6f221b82": {
      "title": "Casual Sessional Academic - Coral Bell School of Asia Pacific Affairs - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia",
      "description": "Casual Sessional Academic - Coral Bell School of Asia Pacific Affairs - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Privacy Statement People & Culture Information for applicants Salaries & benefits Respect and Inclusion Aboriginal & Torres Strait Islander employment Applicant Login Kiosk mode Casual Sessional Academic - Coral Bell School of Asia Pacific Affairs Casual Sessional Academic - Coral Bell School of Asia Pacific Affairs 564097 Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia, 2601 Casual Closing at: Nov 22 2026 - 23:55 AEDT Position Description Classification: Casual Sessional Academic Salary package: As per Casual Sessional Academic salary rates Term: Casual, commencing February 2027 In 1946, ANU was founded to help rebuild a changing world. We’re still at it, 80 years later. For generations, our people have turned curiosity into ideas that have shaped Australia and our region. Learn more about ANU, our history and the community that has shaped us: ANU at 80 We are seeking to appoint one or more Casual Sessional Academics for the courses and roles outlined below. Please note, this may change as student enrolment final numbers become known. The courses listed and the associated CSA activities are indicative only. Area and Discipline courses Semester 1, 2027 PASI1011 – Pacific Encounters: An Introduction to Pacific Studies. Convenor: Rochelle-Lee Bailey (Marking & Meetings) POLS2055 - Pacific Politics. Convenor: Kerryn Baker (Tutorials, marking & meetings) PASI3001 - Politics and Development in the Contemporary Pacific. Convenor: Amanda Watson (Marking) DIPL8013 - Pacific Diplomacy. Convenor: George Carter (Marking) INTR8043 - Changing Geopolitics and International Relations in the Pacific. Convenor: Sonia Palmieri (Marking) INTR1021 - Understanding Peace and Conflict. Convenor: Eglantine Staunton (Tutorials, marking & meetings) INTR2012 - Chinese Foreign and Security Policy. Convenor: Graeme Smith (Lectures, tutorials, marking & meetings) INTR3001 - Humanitarianism: Principles, Politics and Practice. Convenor: Cecilia Jacob (Tutorials, marking & meetings) DIPL8001 - Transnational Diplomacy. Convenor: Lauren Richardson (Marking) INTR8011 - International Relations Theory. Convenor: George Lawson (Marking) DIPL8052 - Key Debates/ Issues in Peace and Conflict Studies. Convenor: Erika Yague (Marking) INTR8068 - Foreign Policy Analysis. Convenor: Ben Day (Marking) INTR8072 - Research Design and Methods in Global Politics. Convenor: Cian O'Driscoll (Marking) INTR8080 – Global Ethics. Convenor: Luke Glanville (Marking) ASIA1035 – Introduction to Asian Politics. Convenor: Nick Cheeseman (Tutorials, marking & meetings) ASIA2111 - Indonesian Foreign and Security Policy. Convenor: Marcus Mietzner (Tutorials, marking & meetings) STST1001 - Introduction to International Security Studies. Convenor: Brendan Taylor (Lectures, tutorials, marking, meetings) STST2001 - Security Concepts in the Asia-Pacific. Convenor: Amy King (Lectures, tutorials, marking, meetings) STST3005 - International Security in the 21st Century. Convenor: Jochen Prantl (Tutorials, marking, meetings) STST8001 - Strategy and Defence in the 21st Century: Concepts and Cases. Convenor: Kyungwon Suh (Marking) STST8026 - Nuclear Strategy. Convenor: Stephan Frühling (Marking) STST8066 - Strategy, Statecraft and Diplomacy for the 21st Century. Convenor: Jochen Prantl (Marking) STST8068 - Asian Strategy and Security: History, Theory and Practice. Convenor: Evelyn Goh (Marking) Autumn Session, 2027 We currently expect to require one or more Casual Sessional Academics for the following courses. These courses will be delivered by ANU staff in Japan as part of the Bell School Dual Degree program with Ritsumeikan University.  The hired CSA's will be located in Canberra. BAPA1003 - Pacific Encounters: An introduction to Pacific Studies. Convenor: Nicholas Hoare (Tutorials, marking & Meetings) BAPA3003 - Politics and Development in the Contemporary Pacific. Convenor: Nicholas Hoare (Tutorials & marking) BAPA1006 - Peacebuilding and Conflict Resolution. Convenor: Catherine Hirst (Tutorials & marking) BAPA3001 - Humanitarianism: Principles, Politics and Practice. Convenor: Catherine Hirst (Tutorials & marking) College overview The ANU College of Asia & the Pacific (CAP) leads intellectual engagement with the Asia-Pacific region through research, teaching and contributions to public debate, and seeks to set the international standard for scholarship concerning the region. The College hosts the largest number of regional experts and specialist academic programs in the English-speaking world and plays a vital role in informing public policy and Australia's intellectual engagement with the societies, cultures and economies of Asia and the Pacific. CAP is the centre of excellence for understanding human development and organisation in Asia and the Pacific, and addressing the cultural, economic, environmental, political and societal dimensions of global challenges. Follow the College of Asia and the Pacific on social media: LinkedIn , Instagram and Facebook Position overview As a casual sessional academic you will be involved in the delivery of CAP courses with support, guidance and direction from the Course Convenor and School staff. You will work both independently and as part of a team to inspire active and evidence-based learning in our courses as well as motivate students to achieve their best and help them meet the stated learning outcomes. A CSA shall have qualification and/or experiences recognised by the institution as appropriate for the relevant discipline area. These include past teaching experience, high achievement in coursework, and employment in related topics to the courses offered by the school. ANU is committed to building a diverse and inclusive community, and particularly welcomes applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people and candidates from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds. Furthermore, it is practice in the ANU College of Asia and the Pacific (CAP) to actively seek a gender mix of shortlisted candidates for interview. For more information about staff equity at ANU, visit https://services.anu.edu.au/human-resources/respect-inclusion We welcome and develop diversity of backgrounds, experiences and ideas and encourage applications from individuals who may have had non-traditional career paths, who may have taken a career break, had career disruptions or who have achieved excellence in careers outside of the higher education sector. We support applicants who require flexible arrangements in their work environments or patterns. If your experience looks a little different to what we’ve described, but you’re passionate and motivated by this position, we welcome your enquiry and application. If you would like to further discuss your application, please contact Business Service Team, Coral Bell School of Asia Pacific Affairs : businessservices.bell@anu.edu.au How to apply Applicants must apply online via the ANU recruitment portal and follow the requirements below. Failure to follow these instructions can lead to immediate failure of your application : 1 page cover letter including the following: Course(s) you would prefer to teach A brief summary of your teaching experience, either at the ANU or another institution and include any relevant industry experience (If you have previously tutored at ANU before, please list courses tutored. Please note that Convenors may be contacted) Your Resume with appropriate length (up to 3 pages) with a list of minimum 2 referees (preferably including a current or previous supervisor) . Complete the MS form . Please ensure you also fill in the MS form and listed down your preference to teach. Link here: Semester 1 & Autum Session 2027- Bell Casual Sessional Academic - Course Preference Form – Fill out form Please note that course availability is subject to change depending on enrolment numbers. Please note: The preferred candidate must have rights to live and work in Australia, and will be required to complete mandatory pre-employment declarations and background checks relevant to the role they have applied for, in accordance with the Background Checking Procedure and the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence . Review of applications will begin after the closing date. Recommended Jobs Case Manager, Accessibility Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Provide disability assessments and coordinate educational support plans, ensuring equitable access and student success. Technical Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical and research support for synthetic biology and crop genetic transformation across diverse research programs. Technical Officer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical support to clinical skills teaching programs, helping facilitate hands-on medical education. Communications Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Lead strategic communications, media and digital content to showcase First Nations research, policy impact and community engagement at ANU. Desktop Support Officer (TechCon Project Officer) Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Support the TechCon project by delivering hands-on desktop support, troubleshooting technical issues and improving IT services across campus. Lecturer/Senior Lecturer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 The School of Literature, Languages and Linguistics is seeking applications from outstanding scholars in the discipline of Computational Linguistics and/or Computational Literary Studies to contribute to its research and teaching program."
    },
    "https://jobs.anu.edu.au/jobs/postdoctoral-fellow-canberra-act-act-australia-4e29bc16-71b0-41f9-8783-d2c9f5525fa2": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow  - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Privacy Statement People & Culture Information for applicants Salaries & benefits Respect and Inclusion Aboriginal & Torres Strait Islander employment Applicant Login Kiosk mode Postdoctoral Fellow Postdoctoral Fellow 563088 Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia, 2601 Fixed Term Closing at: Dec 6 2026 - 23:55 AEDT Position Description Classification : Academic Level A Salary package : $91,546 - $114,906 per annum plus 17% superannuation Terms : Full time, Fixed term (up to 24 months) In 1946, ANU was founded to help rebuild a changing world. We’re still at it, 80 years later. For generations, our people have turned curiosity into ideas that have shaped Australia and our region. Learn more about ANU, our history and the community that has shaped us: ANU at 80 The Position The Research School of Earth Sciences is seeking a Postdoctoral Fellow to undertake research on the Serra Negra Rare Earth Element (REE) Prospect in Brazil. Working within a leading geochemistry research team, you will investigate mineralisation processes through field studies, advanced geochemical and isotopic analysis, and data interpretation. The role offers the opportunity to publish high-impact research, collaborate with national and international researchers, and contribute to student supervision within a dynamic academic environment. The Person You will hold a PhD in Geochemistry, Petrology, or a related discipline, with expertise in critical mineral deposits and geochemical analytical techniques. You will have a strong track record of research, excellent communication skills, and the ability to work collaboratively across multidisciplinary teams. Experience supporting or supervising student research projects will be highly regarded. The School The Research School of Earth Sciences is Australia's leading Earth science research institution, recognised internationally for innovation in experimental, analytical, and computational approaches to understanding Earth processes and resources. The College The ANU College of Science and Medicine brings together world-class researchers across disciplines including Earth Sciences, Physics, Biology, Chemistry, Astronomy, Medicine and Psychology. The College delivers research-led education and internationally recognised scientific discovery supported by outstanding facilities and global collaborations. For further information please contact Professor John Mavrogenes, Research School of Earth Science, E: John.Mavrogenes@anu.edu.au The University actively encourages applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. For more information on employment opportunities, contact our Indigenous Employment Consultant on indigenous.employment@anu.edu.au ANU values diversity and inclusion and is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to those of all backgrounds and identities. For more information about staff equity at ANU, visit https://services.anu.edu.au/human-resources/respect-inclusion Application information In order to apply for this role please make sure that you upload the following documents: A statement addressing the selection criteria. A current curriculum vitae (CV) which includes the names and contact details of at least three referees (preferably including a current or previous supervisor). If your CV does not include referees you can complete these online when prompted in the application form. Other documents, if required. Applications which do not address the selection criteria may not be considered for the position. Please note: The preferred candidate will be required to complete mandatory pre-employment declarations and background checks relevant to the role they have applied for, in accordance with the Background Checking Procedure and the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence . Recommended Jobs Case Manager, Accessibility Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Provide disability assessments and coordinate educational support plans, ensuring equitable access and student success. Technical Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical and research support for synthetic biology and crop genetic transformation across diverse research programs. Technical Officer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical support to clinical skills teaching programs, helping facilitate hands-on medical education. Communications Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Lead strategic communications, media and digital content to showcase First Nations research, policy impact and community engagement at ANU. Desktop Support Officer (TechCon Project Officer) Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Support the TechCon project by delivering hands-on desktop support, troubleshooting technical issues and improving IT services across campus. Lecturer/Senior Lecturer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 The School of Literature, Languages and Linguistics is seeking applications from outstanding scholars in the discipline of Computational Linguistics and/or Computational Literary Studies to contribute to its research and teaching program."
    },
    "https://jobs.anu.edu.au/jobs/academic-convener-canberra-act-act-australia-7e292526-6f07-496b-941a-d9429d4e2240": {
      "title": "Academic Convener - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia",
      "description": "Academic Convener - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Privacy Statement People & Culture Information for applicants Salaries & benefits Respect and Inclusion Aboriginal & Torres Strait Islander employment Applicant Login Kiosk mode Academic Convener Academic Convener 564062 Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia, 2601 Fixed Term Closing at: Nov 11 2026 - 23:55 AEDT Position Description Classification: Academic Level C Salary package: $149,657–$166,338 per annum plus 17% superannuation Terms: Full-time, Fixed term (until 30 November 2029) Make a meaningful contribution to Pacific security through academic leadership at ANU’s Pacific Security College. Shape and deliver education and professional development programs that respond to Pacific priorities. Join a collaborative team and build partnerships with governments, universities and regional institutions across the Pacific. In 1946, ANU was founded to help rebuild a changing world. We’re still at it, 80 years later. For generations, our people have turned curiosity into ideas that have shaped Australia and our region. Learn more about ANU, our history and the community that has shaped us: ANU at 80. Position overview The Pacific Security College is funded by the Australian Government and is part of the Crawford School of Public Policy within the ANU College of Law, Governance and Policy at the Australian National University. Reporting to the Director, the Academic Convener will provide academic leadership in the design, delivery and ongoing improvement of the College’s education and professional development programs. The role will work closely with colleagues and Pacific partners to ensure programs are academically rigorous, practically relevant and responsive to the region’s security priorities. The Academic Convener will develop curricula and learning materials, convene and deliver courses and workshops, coordinate contributions from academic and practitioner experts and evaluate learning outcomes. The role will connect research, policy and practice to strengthen the capacity of Pacific officials to address complex security challenges. The successful candidate will contribute to the College’s research, policy engagement and academic partnerships, working collaboratively with governments, universities and regional institutions. Travel to Pacific island countries will be required. While the position has a core set of responsibilities, there will be opportunities for the successful candidate to contribute in ways that align with their interests, expertise and professional development goals. The Person You will bring a PhD in a relevant discipline, or equivalent qualifications and professional experience, together with expertise in Pacific security, public policy, international relations or a related field. You will have demonstrated experience designing, convening and delivering high-quality education or professional development programs. You will be able to translate complex research and policy issues into accessible, engaging learning experiences for diverse audiences, including government officials and practitioners. You will bring a strong record of research, policy engagement or professional contributions relevant to the role, consistent with appointment at Academic Level C. You will work collaboratively and build productive relationships with Pacific stakeholders, academic colleagues and practitioner networks. An understanding of Pacific contexts and the ability to work respectfully across cultures are essential. The ability to exercise initiative and good judgement, coordinate contributions from others, manage competing priorities and deliver high-quality outcomes within agreed timeframes is essential. The College The Pacific Security College is funded by the Australian Government and is part of the Crawford School of Public Policy within the ANU College of Law, Governance and Policy at the Australian National University. The Pacific Security College works with Pacific island countries to build the capacity of officials to address a broad range of security challenges through executive and professional development courses, policy workshops, technical assistance and research. We take a holistic approach to Pacific security, encompassing traditional and non-traditional security issues and guided by the Pacific Islands Forum’s Boe Declaration on Regional Security , the 2050 Strategy for the Blue Pacific Continent and the Blue Pacific Ocean of Peace Declaration . We work in a spirit of service and collaboration with our Pacific stakeholders in national governments, universities, civil society organisations and regional institutions. For further information please contact Pacific Security College Director Professor Dave Peebles, E: Dave. Peebles@anu.edu.au ANU values diversity and inclusion and is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to people of all backgrounds and identities. For enquiries about the application process, or if you require reasonable adjustment to apply for this role, please contact the hiring manager using the details above. Application information In order to apply for this role please make sure that you upload the following documents: A statement addressing the selection criteria. A current curriculum vitae (CV) which includes the names and contact details of at least three referees (preferably including a current or previous supervisor). If your CV does not include referees you can complete these online when prompted in the application form. Other documents, if required. Applications which do not address the selection criteria may not be considered for the position. Please note: The preferred candidate must have rights to live and work in Australia, and will be required to complete mandatory pre-employment declarations and background checks relevant to the role they have applied for, in accordance with the Background Checking Procedure and the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence . Recommended Jobs Case Manager, Accessibility Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Provide disability assessments and coordinate educational support plans, ensuring equitable access and student success. Technical Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical and research support for synthetic biology and crop genetic transformation across diverse research programs. Technical Officer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical support to clinical skills teaching programs, helping facilitate hands-on medical education. Communications Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Lead strategic communications, media and digital content to showcase First Nations research, policy impact and community engagement at ANU. Desktop Support Officer (TechCon Project Officer) Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Support the TechCon project by delivering hands-on desktop support, troubleshooting technical issues and improving IT services across campus. Lecturer/Senior Lecturer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 The School of Literature, Languages and Linguistics is seeking applications from outstanding scholars in the discipline of Computational Linguistics and/or Computational Literary Studies to contribute to its research and teaching program."
    },
    "https://jobs.anu.edu.au/jobs/research-fellow-computer-science-canberra-act-act-australia-e5f4c9bf-f474-460b-bce6-fbdc9b3b5b3a": {
      "title": "Research Fellow (Computer Science)",
      "description": "Research Fellow (Computer Science) - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Privacy Statement People & Culture Information for applicants Salaries & benefits Respect and Inclusion Aboriginal & Torres Strait Islander employment Applicant Login Kiosk mode Research Fellow (Computer Science) Research Fellow (Computer Science) 563823 Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia, 2601 Fixed Term Closing at: Nov 22 2026 - 23:55 AEDT Position Description Classification: Academic Level B Salary package: $124,638 - $141,317 per annum plus 17% superannuation Term: Full time, Fixed Term (12 months) Engage with world-class computing infrastructure and work in partnership with the Australian government Develop sophisticated AI approaches intersecting with psychology and computer science In 1946, ANU was founded to help rebuild a changing world. We’re still at it, 80 years later. For generations, our people have turned curiosity into ideas that have shaped Australia and our region. Learn more about ANU, our history and the community that has shaped us: ANU at 80 Position Overview This post-doc represents an exciting collaboration between ANU and the Australian government, focussing on the intersection between AI and personality, with a specific focus on understanding personality in an online environment. To solve this problem, you will work closely with a fellow psychology post-doctoral candidate. Specifically, this role will seek to develop sophisticated AI approaches that extract psychological insights from digital text data. You will work with world-class computing infrastructure while tackling challenging problems that bridge computer science and psychology, collaborating directly with both academic researchers and the Australian Government. You will fine-tune and build orchestration pipelines for pre-trained and large language models (LLMs) to achieve these objectives. Furthermore, you will systematically evaluate and compare alternative orchestration frameworks and agent-based architectures to solve these challenges. Working in partnership with the Australian government, you will have the opportunity to liaise with international partners and academic institutions.  You will also be encouraged to work on additional projects that complement the main body of research, integrate within existing research labs, and develop teaching and science communication skills. The Person We are looking for a qualified computer scientist or computational linguist with demonstrated expertise in training large language models and using larger scale computing infrastructure for affective computing or applications in psychology. Demonstrated experience designing or deploying multi-model orchestration layers is advantageous. We are looking for someone who likes challenging problems and encourages team members and colleagues to workshop effective solutions across disciplinary boundaries. Innovation and creativity are essential in this role, and a desire to tackle complex problems that have direct practical application to the work of the Australian government. This role requires access to sensitive research environments. The successful applicant must be eligible for, and willing to undergo, an Australian Government security clearance. Continued engagement in the role is subject to obtaining and maintaining this clearance. Learn more about the ground-breaking research and world class facilities of the Research School of Medicine and Psychology. For more information about the position please contact Dr Conal Monaghan at E: conal.monaghan@anu.edu.au . The University actively encourages applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. For more information on employment opportunities, contact our Indigenous Employment Consultant on indigenous.employment@anu.edu.au ANU values diversity and inclusion and is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to those of all backgrounds and identities. For more information about staff equity at ANU, visit https://services.anu.edu.au/human-resources/respect-inclusion Application Information To be considered for this position, please upload the following documents: A current curriculum vitae (CV), A short statement indicating your interest and motivation for this role (no more than 300 words) No more than a four-page summary addressing your suitability against the selection criteria. If shortlisted, candidates will be asked to complete a: •             30-minute interview using with the supervisory panel Applications which do not address the selection criteria may not be considered for the position. Please note: The preferred candidate must have rights to live and work in Australia and will be required to complete mandatory pre-employment declarations and background checks relevant to the role they have applied for, in accordance with the Background Checking Procedure and the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence . Recommended Jobs Case Manager, Accessibility Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Provide disability assessments and coordinate educational support plans, ensuring equitable access and student success. Technical Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical and research support for synthetic biology and crop genetic transformation across diverse research programs. Technical Officer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical support to clinical skills teaching programs, helping facilitate hands-on medical education. Communications Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Lead strategic communications, media and digital content to showcase First Nations research, policy impact and community engagement at ANU. Desktop Support Officer (TechCon Project Officer) Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Support the TechCon project by delivering hands-on desktop support, troubleshooting technical issues and improving IT services across campus. Lecturer/Senior Lecturer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 The School of Literature, Languages and Linguistics is seeking applications from outstanding scholars in the discipline of Computational Linguistics and/or Computational Literary Studies to contribute to its research and teaching program."
    },
    "https://jobs.anu.edu.au/jobs/postdoctoral-fellow-canberra-act-act-australia-0d7e2252-70c9-42bf-bee2-f8e3681e8f86": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Privacy Statement People & Culture Information for applicants Salaries & benefits Respect and Inclusion Aboriginal & Torres Strait Islander employment Applicant Login Kiosk mode Postdoctoral Fellow Postdoctoral Fellow 563957 Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia, 2601 Fixed Term Closing at: Dec 1 2026 - 23:55 AEDT Position Description Classification: Academic Level A Salary package: $91,546 - $114,906 per annum plus 17% superannuation Terms: Full-time, fixed-term appointment for up to 2 years In 1946, ANU was founded to help rebuild a changing world. We’re still at it, 80 years later. For generations, our people have turned curiosity into ideas that have shaped Australia and our region. Learn more about ANU, our history and the community that has shaped us: ANU at 80 The Position The Position supports a National Health and Medical Research Council Ideas Grant that seeks to test a delivery system for the release of therapeutics in the gut to prevent urinary tracts infections. The project will involve mouse experimentation, metagenomics, and RNAseq analyses. A key part of the role will be training and mentoring 2-3 Honours and/or PhD students and supervising undergraduate students on related projects. The Person Applicants should have demonstratable experience in experimentation employing mouse models of human disease. Experience in shotgun metagenomics by long-red (Nanopore) sequencing, workflow for metagenome-assembled genome synthesis, metagenome classification and analysis, taxonomic comparisons, and/or transcriptomics, will be viewed favourably but is not essential. We seek a friendly and self-motivated individual with excellent interpersonal skills who takes initiative and who can work both independently and collaboratively to achieve common goals and solve challenging problems. The successful applicant will work closely with Dr Leyton, Dr Furlong and Professor Schwessinger to ensure that the milestones of the NHMRC-funded project are met. The School/Department The Research School of Biology is a leading centre of biomedical science and biochemistry research in Australia where researchers have a tradition of excellence in addressing the world’s most pressing issues, including infections caused by bacterial pathogens. The College/Division The ANU College of Science and Medicine encompasses the disciplines of: Astronomy and Astrophysics, Biology, Chemistry, Earth Sciences, Medical Research, Medicine and Psychology, Physics. Staff and students within the ANU College of Science and Medicine conduct research and deliver a research-led education program that encompasses the entire breadth of the sciences, supported by extensive international networks and by world-class facilities. For further information please contact, Dr Denisse Leyton: denisse.leyton@anu.edu.au The University actively encourages applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. For more information on employment opportunities, contact our Indigenous Employment Consultant on indigenous.employment@anu.edu.au ANU values diversity and inclusion and is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to people of all backgrounds and identities.  For more information about staff equity at ANU, click here . For enquiries about the application process, or if you require reasonable adjustment to apply for this role, please contact the hiring manager on the above listed details. Application information To be considered for this position, please upload the following documents: (No more than) a two-page summary addressing your suitability against the selection criteria, A current curriculum vitae (CV) which includes the names and contact details of at least three referees (preferably including a current or previous supervisor). If your CV does not include referees, you can complete these online when prompted in the application form, and Provide links for two recently published papers that you have read and found compelling, along with brief (2-3 sentence) explanations of why the work excited you. Applications which do not address the selection criteria may not be considered for the position. Please note: The preferred candidate will be required to complete mandatory pre-employment declarations and background checks relevant to the role they have applied for, in accordance with the Background Checking Procedure and the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence . Apply Now First Name (required) f456f481 Last Name (required) 8d34d77a Email (required) b05397a4 Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Job referral Know someone great for this role! Recommended Jobs Case Manager, Accessibility Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Provide disability assessments and coordinate educational support plans, ensuring equitable access and student success. Technical Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical and research support for synthetic biology and crop genetic transformation across diverse research programs. Technical Officer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical support to clinical skills teaching programs, helping facilitate hands-on medical education. Communications Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Lead strategic communications, media and digital content to showcase First Nations research, policy impact and community engagement at ANU. Desktop Support Officer (TechCon Project Officer) Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Support the TechCon project by delivering hands-on desktop support, troubleshooting technical issues and improving IT services across campus. Lecturer/Senior Lecturer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 The School of Literature, Languages and Linguistics is seeking applications from outstanding scholars in the discipline of Computational Linguistics and/or Computational Literary Studies to contribute to its research and teaching program. Candidate Details This website uses cookies. We use cookies to personalise content such as job recommendations, and to analyse our traffic. You consent to our cookies if you click \"I Accept\". If you click on \"I Do Not Accept\", then we will not use cookies but you may have a deteriorated user experience. You can change your settings by clicking on the Settings link on the top right of the device I do not accept I accept"
