    "https://jobs.anu.edu.au/jobs/senior-research-and-engagement-fellow-canberra-act-act-australia": {
      "title": "Senior Research and Engagement Fellow",
      "description": "Senior Research and Engagement Fellow - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Privacy Statement People & Culture Information for applicants Salaries & benefits Respect and Inclusion Aboriginal & Torres Strait Islander employment Applicant Login Kiosk mode Senior Research and Engagement Fellow Senior Research and Engagement Fellow 564063 Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia, 2601 Fixed Term Closing at: Dec 1 2026 - 23:55 AEDT Position Description Classification: Academic Level C Salary package: $149,657–$166,338 per annum plus 17% superannuation Terms: Full-time, fixed-term (until 30 November 2029) Make a meaningful contribution to Pacific security through research and policy engagement at ANU’s Pacific Security College. Lead research that responds to Pacific priorities and translates evidence into practical policy advice. Join a collaborative team and build partnerships with governments, universities and regional institutions across the Pacific. In 1946, ANU was founded to help rebuild a changing world. We’re still at it, 80 years later. For generations, our people have turned curiosity into ideas that have shaped Australia and our region. Learn more about ANU, our history and the community that has shaped us: ANU at 80. Position overview The Pacific Security College is funded by the Australian Government and is part of the Crawford School of Public Policy within the ANU College of Law, Governance and Policy at the Australian National University. Reporting to the Director, the Senior Research and Engagement Fellow will lead and undertake research and policy engagement that support the College’s work with Pacific island countries. The role will work closely with colleagues and Pacific partners to identify research priorities and deliver analysis that is academically rigorous, practically relevant and responsive to the region’s security challenges. The Senior Research and Engagement Fellow will develop and deliver research projects, produce academic publications and policy-focused outputs and communicate findings to diverse audiences. The role will connect research, policy and practice through policy dialogue, workshops and engagement with government officials, regional organisations and academic institutions. The successful candidate will build and maintain research and stakeholder partnerships, support collaborative research with Pacific scholars and practitioners and contribute expertise to the College’s education, professional development and technical assistance activities. Travel to Pacific island countries will be required. While the position has a core set of responsibilities, there will be opportunities for the successful candidate to contribute in ways that align with their interests, expertise and professional development goals. The Person You will bring a PhD in a relevant discipline, or equivalent qualifications and professional experience, together with expertise in Pacific security, public policy, international relations or a related field. You will have a strong record of high-quality research and publications, together with demonstrated experience translating research into accessible policy advice and practical outcomes, consistent with appointment at Academic Level C. You will have demonstrated experience developing and managing research projects, applying appropriate research methods and delivering high-quality outputs within agreed timeframes. You will be able to identify emerging issues and develop research that responds to the priorities of Pacific partners. You will bring strong communication and engagement skills, including the ability to present complex findings clearly, facilitate policy discussions and build productive relationships with government officials, researchers and practitioners. You will work collaboratively and demonstrate an understanding of Pacific contexts, respect for Pacific knowledge and perspectives and the ability to work effectively across cultures. The ability to exercise initiative and good judgement, manage competing priorities and contribute to a supportive and collaborative team environment is essential. The College The Pacific Security College is funded by the Australian Government and is part of the Crawford School of Public Policy within the ANU College of Law, Governance and Policy at the Australian National University. The Pacific Security College works with Pacific island countries to build the capacity of officials to address a broad range of security challenges through executive and professional development courses, policy workshops, technical assistance and research. We take a holistic approach to Pacific security, encompassing traditional and non-traditional security issues and guided by the Pacific Islands Forum’s Boe Declaration on Regional Security , the 2050 Strategy for the Blue Pacific Continent and the Blue Pacific Ocean of Peace Declaration . We work in a spirit of service and collaboration with our Pacific stakeholders in national governments, universities, civil society organisations and regional institutions. For further information please contact Pacific Security College Director Professor Dave Peebles, E: Dave.Peebles@anu.edu.au ANU values diversity and inclusion and is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to people of all backgrounds and identities. For enquiries about the application process, or if you require reasonable adjustment to apply for this role, please contact the hiring manager using the details above. Application information In order to apply for this role please make sure that you upload the following documents: A statement addressing the selection criteria. A current curriculum vitae (CV) which includes the names and contact details of at least three referees (preferably including a current or previous supervisor). If your CV does not include referees you can complete these online when prompted in the application form. Other documents, if required. Applications which do not address the selection criteria may not be considered for the position. Please note: The preferred candidate will be required to complete mandatory pre-employment declarations and background checks relevant to the role they have applied for, in accordance with the Background Checking Procedure and the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence . Recommended Jobs Case Manager, Accessibility Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Provide disability assessments and coordinate educational support plans, ensuring equitable access and student success. Technical Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical and research support for synthetic biology and crop genetic transformation across diverse research programs. Technical Officer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical support to clinical skills teaching programs, helping facilitate hands-on medical education. Communications Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Lead strategic communications, media and digital content to showcase First Nations research, policy impact and community engagement at ANU. Desktop Support Officer (TechCon Project Officer) Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Support the TechCon project by delivering hands-on desktop support, troubleshooting technical issues and improving IT services across campus. Lecturer/Senior Lecturer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 The School of Literature, Languages and Linguistics is seeking applications from outstanding scholars in the discipline of Computational Linguistics and/or Computational Literary Studies to contribute to its research and teaching program. Candidate Details This website uses cookies. We use cookies to personalise content such as job recommendations, and to analyse our traffic. You consent to our cookies if you click \"I Accept\". If you click on \"I Do Not Accept\", then we will not use cookies but you may have a deteriorated user experience. You can change your settings by clicking on the Settings link on the top right of the device I do not accept I accept"
    },
    "https://jobs.anu.edu.au/jobs/lecturer-senior-lecturer-canberra-act-act-australia-0ca4ff75-9d9a-409e-a62c-ed1832cad383": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer",
      "description": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Privacy Statement People & Culture Information for applicants Salaries & benefits Respect and Inclusion Aboriginal & Torres Strait Islander employment Applicant Login Kiosk mode Lecturer/Senior Lecturer Lecturer/Senior Lecturer 564199 Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia, 2601 Continuing Closing at: Dec 6 2026 - 23:55 AEDT Position Description Classification: Academic Level B or C Salary package: $124,638 - $166,338 per annum plus 17% superannuation Terms: Full time, Continuing In 1946, ANU was founded to help rebuild a changing world. We’re still at it, 80 years later. For generations, our people have turned curiosity into ideas that have shaped Australia and our region. Learn more about ANU, our history and the community that has shaped us: ANU at 80 The Position The School of Literature, Languages and Linguistics (SLLL) is seeking to appoint a Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Computational Linguistics and/or Computational Literary Studies to strengthen its research and teaching at the intersection of computational methods and the study of literature and language. The Person The successful candidate will pursue an active research agenda applying computational approaches to literary and/or language corpora. They will contribute to teaching across the School’s undergraduate and postgraduate coursework programs, including the development of new courses and supervision of Honours, Masters and HDR students. Expertise is sought in one or more of the following areas: computational literary studies; quantitative and computational text analysis; computational linguistics and language technology; advanced quantitative modelling of language and related processes; critical application of and approaches to AI/LLMs; and the construction and analysis of large textual corpora. The School/Department The School of Literature, Languages and Linguistics (SLLL) focuses on teaching and research across a broad range of disciplines related to language, culture, and communication. Key areas include literature, film, drama, and creative writing; gender and cultural studies; classics and ancient history; digital humanities; modern European and classical languages; linguistics and Australian indigenous languages. The School of Literature, Languages and Linguistic combines the study of language, literature, culture, and linguistics with a strong focus on critical thinking, communication skills, intercultural understanding, and research excellence, preparing students for both professional careers and advanced academic work. The College/Division The ANU College of Arts and Social Sciences (CASS) is one of six Colleges at ANU. The College, which is structured into two main research schools, offers degrees in more than 20 discipline areas and excels in research across the creative arts, humanities and social sciences. The College has a substantial international research presence and is a major source of national policy advice. Our academic staff are internationally recognised for their research, and 57 are members of the Australian Academy of the Humanities, the Academy of the Social Sciences of Australia, or both. We also host 13 Australian Research Council Future Fellows and three ARC Laureates. A hub of vibrant activity, we host more than 270 lectures, concerts and exhibitions each year, most of which are open to the public. Our students, staff and graduates come from more than 60 nations, bringing a diversity of perspective to campus life. For further information please contact, Professor Kate Mitchell, T: +61 2 6125 9517, E: kate.mitchell@anu.edu.au The University actively encourages applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. For more information on employment opportunities, contact our Indigenous Employment Consultant on indigenous.employment@anu.edu.au ANU values diversity and inclusion and is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to people of all backgrounds and identities.  For more information about staff equity at ANU, click here . For enquiries about the application process, or if you require reasonable adjustment to apply for this role, please contact the hiring manager on the above listed details. Application information In order to apply for this role please make sure that you upload the following documents: A statement addressing the selection criteria. A current curriculum vitae (CV) which includes the names and contact details of at least three referees (preferably including a current or previous supervisor). If your CV does not include referees you can complete these online when prompted in the application form. Other documents, if required. Applications which do not address the selection criteria may not be considered for the position. Please note: The preferred candidate will be required to complete mandatory pre-employment declarations and background checks relevant to the role they have applied for, in accordance with the Background Checking Procedure and the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence . Recommended Jobs Case Manager, Accessibility Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Provide disability assessments and coordinate educational support plans, ensuring equitable access and student success. Technical Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical and research support for synthetic biology and crop genetic transformation across diverse research programs. Technical Officer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Deliver technical support to clinical skills teaching programs, helping facilitate hands-on medical education. Communications Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Lead strategic communications, media and digital content to showcase First Nations research, policy impact and community engagement at ANU. Desktop Support Officer (TechCon Project Officer) Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Support the TechCon project by delivering hands-on desktop support, troubleshooting technical issues and improving IT services across campus. Production Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Coordinate events, concerts and venue operations at ANU, supporting bookings, resources, customer service and smooth event delivery. Candidate Details This website uses cookies. We use cookies to personalise content such as job recommendations, and to analyse our traffic. You consent to our cookies if you click \"I Accept\". If you click on \"I Do Not Accept\", then we will not use cookies but you may have a deteriorated user experience. You can change your settings by clicking on the Settings link on the top right of the device I do not accept I accept"
